# Session affinity (replaces Julay Channel values)

- `session` transition/constructor modifier marks exclusive pairwise protocols.
- `session` is incompatible with `service` and `internal`; all offers of an action must agree on the session tag.
- Affinity and SyncChannel sessions are **per-Proc local** only — never a Program-global registry.
- First contact: peers rendezvous on the static (global) action channel; Program creates exactly one dedicated SyncChannel in `SyncPayload.sessionToInstall`. Subsequent offers use that dedicated channel. Dedicated syncs install nothing.
- Session constructor: sync size 1 on the global channel; spawn installs follow-on dedicated sessions before the child starts.
- Session constructor exclusivity: while affinity to a peer of that child class is live, a second session-ctor spawn throws `JulayException` (no implicit guards; action stays user-enabled). After peer exit closes sessions and scrub clears affinity, a later spawn may establish again.
- Session key: `(peerProcId, sessionActionName)` → dedicated `SyncChannel` (sync size 2).
- Peer exit closes shared dedicated channels; surviving Select exits on close, scrubs affinity, and may re-handshake on the global channel. Proc exits only when no actions are enabled.
- Untagged default = ordinary non-sticky rendezvous; `service` = shared multiplexed API.

## TLA+ encoding

- Two-sided session specs emit `session_*`, `CanStartSession_*`, `EndSession_*`, plus `sessionException` (init FALSE) and `SessionIntegrity == ~sessionException` (always checked in the `.cfg`).
- Transition-only session actions: sticky `(session \/ CanStart)` under `\* Session connection semantics`.
- Session ctor-bearing (hybrid) actions: under the same comment, `IF CanStart THEN` session assign + spawn updates `ELSE sessionException' = TRUE` (no spawn) — mirrors runtime `JulayException` on rebind.
